let's touch on on those passenger  numbers uh first of all if we can just  break down for us where we stand  relative to those pre-pandemic Peaks  well we're still currently about 25  below where we were pre-pandemic  actually at the moment that's more like  15 to 20 below because the market has  been recovering  um through the second half of the year  but I think it'll take us until 2025  2026 before we get back to the full  level of demand that we saw before the  pandemic and what's played catch up  quicker leisure travel or business  travel short haul or Long Haul  it's been very much about leisure travel  so what we saw as soon as Markets  started to open in March everyone wanted  to catch up on the holidays they hadn't  been able to have for the last couple of  years so we saw a lot of people going  away for weekend breaks uh holiday  season's very busy and this so the Peaks  have been very busy some peak days have  been as busy as they were pre-pandemic  but outside of those peak times it is  much quieter and that's why we are  encouraging all of the companies that  work at Heathrow to provide services to  scale up back to the full capacity they  had before the pandemic so that we can  meet Peak demand whenever that may come  and let's talk about that balance  because clearly one of the reasons uh  this industry and uh you're part of it  in particular hasn't got closer to  pre-pandemic levels has been the supply  not not not the demand and some of those  limits that you are putting in place on  passenger travel numbers that's that  limit is coming off is it very soon but  but might it have to come back on as we  get to the busy Christmas period  yes if I just give you some context so  before the pandemic about 75 000 people  worked at Heathrow ten percent of them  worked directly for us as the airport  operator and the rest for Airlines and  ground handlers and during the Pandemic  those numbers fell by about 25 000  people so down to about 50 000 this time  last year and what we've been  encouraging people to do and we've been  doing ourselves is to scale up as fast  as we can to get back to full capacity  and what we found before the summer peak  in at the end of June beginning of July  was that the demand was slightly ahead  of where the capacity was at that time  so we've put a cap in place and what  that did was to protect people's  Journeys over the summer so we had 18  million people uh traveling over the  summer service levels actually close to  where they were in 2019 so that's a real  achievement by all of my colleagues here  at Heathrow to serve our passengers but  we're not yet back to where we were  three pandemic so we we have a cap in  place it's coming off uh in the next few  days it actually hasn't affected any  travel over the last few months because  demand has been below the level of the  cap but we think that there may be some  peak days over Christmas and maybe even  into next year where we may have demand  slightly ahead of Supply so we're just  going to have a mechanism in the  background but you won't notice if  you're a traveler that will just make  sure that we can guarantee a smooth  service when you're traveling through  Heathrow I'm not sure John what what  metric allows you to say that service  levels were were as good this summer as  in past years I'm not sure that was all  consumers experience but parked that  issue to the to the side I think it's  fair to say over the summer that  Airlines themselves took the brunt of  the criticism did you feel sorry for  them I get that it's a shared problem  not a Heathrow Airport problem but given  that you had imposed the passenger cap  yourselves  well it was a difficult situation for  everyone and this this is a shared  problem not just a Heathrow actually but  around the world and uh I think every  part of the world has had challenges in  scaling up quickly particularly those  that were worst hit  um by covid restrictions and the UK was  worse hit than  um our competitors in Europe uh but  actually we have worked together with  Airlines and their ground handlers to  build back capacity we have a very  collaborative Approach at the airport  because if you're traveling you don't  care who is providing service you just  want to have a seamless journey and  that's part of my job as the airport  operator to coordinate all of those 400  companies on the airport to give you a  seamless service and to step in if need  be to make sure that we can keep supply  and demand in balance that was a  difficult choice we made before the  summer we had been talking with Airlines  about it beforehand I do feel a lot of  sympathy for Airlines and passengers who  had some disruption when we first  brought the cap in but thereafter  actually we gave very good service  through the summer and the measure I  look at is what passengers are telling  me not just when I'm working in the  terminals myself and people are getting  through check-in and security far  quicker than they expected but also when  I look at the survey results the show  that we were in terms of Passenger  satisfaction very close to the high  levels of service that we saw back in  2019  and and John just finally in terms of  really solving this issue you know  medium and long term is it all down to  being able to find the right the right  staff given understandably that your  industry cut staff during during the  pandemic and building back up has been  harder than the process of of perhaps  letting people off are there any changes  you'd like to see from the government  the authorities to make hiring easier I  mean it's an industry where of course we  want safety first so maybe that makes  that tricky  well you're exactly right it is it's  just a scale challenge we're going from  50 000 people to 75 000 people uh we're  about halfway there at the moment we're  all working super hard you've got to  recruit the right people with a good  service culture you've got to get them  through security clearance to make sure  that they are not a a security risk and  then you've got to train them up in the  specific skills they need many very  skill jobs uh across the airport and it  just takes time to physically do all of  that and no matter how hard you work at  it the one thing government has helped  us with but could help us with more is  on the security clearance side and the  main thing that takes time is validating  people's employment history over the  last five years and if the government  could give us easier access to the hmrc  data records which which has a lot of  the information that we need that would  make it quicker to get through security  clearance and where that helps is that a  lot of people of course are applying for  jobs at other employers other than on  the airport and if they get their their  job being able to start before they've  got the security clearance for Heathrow  then we lose them from the whole process  so we have to start again in recruiting  a good person so cutting down the  security clearance time without losing  any level of security would really help  us
 

